    "id": 126,
    "type": "multi_choice",
    "domain": "5.0",
    "domainName": "Security Program Management and Oversight",
    "domainName_uz": "Dastur Boshqaruvi va Nazorat",
    "q_en": "A company is concerned about weather events causing damage to the server room and downtime. Which of\nthe following should the company consider?",
    "q_uz": "A company is concerned about weather events causing damage to the server room and downtime. Which of\nthe following should the company consider?",
    "options_en": [
      "Clustering servers",
      "Geographic dispersion",
      "Load balancers",
      "Off-site backups"
    ],
    "options_uz": [
      "Clustering servers",
      "Geographic dispersion",
      "Load balancers",
      "Off-site backups"
    ],
    "correct": [
      1
    ],
    "selectCount": 2,
    "explanation_en": "Geographic dispersion is a strategy that involves distributing the servers or data centers across different\ngeographic locations. Geographic dispersion can help the company to mitigate the risk of weather events\ncausing damage to the server room and downtime, as well as improve the availability, performance, and\nresilience of the network. Geographic dispersion can also enhance the disaster recovery and business\ncontinuity capabilities of the company, as it can provide backup and failover options in case of a regional\noutage or disruption 12.\nThe other options are not the best ways to address the company's concern:\nClustering servers: This is a technique that involves grouping multiple servers together to act as a single\nsystem. Clustering servers can help to improve the performance, scalability, and fault tolerance of the\nnetwork, but it does not protect the servers from physical damage or downtime caused by weather events,\nespecially if the servers are located in the same room or building3.\nLoad balancers: These are devices or software that distribute the network traffic or workload among multiple\nservers or resources. Load balancers can help to optimize the utilization, efficiency, and reliability of the\nnetwork, but they do not prevent the servers from being damaged or disrupted by weather events, especially if\nthe servers are located in the same room or building4.\nOff-site backups: These are copies of data or files that are stored in a different location than the original\nsource. Off-site backups can help to protect the data from being lost or corrupted by weather events, but they\ndo not prevent the servers from being damaged or disrupted by weather events, nor do they ensure the\navailability or continuity of the network services.\nReferences = 1: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 972: High Availability -\nCompTIA Security+ SY0-701 – 3.4, video by Professor Messer3: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification\nStudy Guide, page 984: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 99.: CompTIA\nSecurity+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 100.",
    "explanation_uz": "Geographic dispersion is a strategy that involves distributing the servers or data centers across different\ngeographic locations. Geographic dispersion can help the company to mitigate the risk of weather events\ncausing damage to the server room and downtime, as well as improve the availability, performance, and\nresilience of the network. Geographic dispersion can also enhance the disaster recovery and business\ncontinuity capabilities of the company, as it can provide backup and failover options in case of a regional\noutage or disruption 12.\nThe other options are not the best ways to address the company's concern:\nClustering servers: This is a technique that involves grouping multiple servers together to act as a single\nsystem. Clustering servers can help to improve the performance, scalability, and fault tolerance of the\nnetwork, but it does not protect the servers from physical damage or downtime caused by weather events,\nespecially if the servers are located in the same room or building3.\nLoad balancers: These are devices or software that distribute the network traffic or workload among multiple\nservers or resources. Load balancers can help to optimize the utilization, efficiency, and reliability of the\nnetwork, but they do not prevent the servers from being damaged or disrupted by weather events, especially if\nthe servers are located in the same room or building4.\nOff-site backups: These are copies of data or files that are stored in a different location than the original\nsource. Off-site backups can help to protect the data from being lost or corrupted by weather events, but they\ndo not prevent the servers from being damaged or disrupted by weather events, nor do they ensure the\navailability or continuity of the network services.\n\nManba: 1: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 972: High Availability -\nCompTIA Security+ SY0-701 – 3.4, video by Professor Messer3: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification\nStudy Guide, page 984: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 99.: CompTIA\nSecurity+ SY0-701 Certification Study Guide, page 100."
